Z||000000||fiero56||12-06-1999||03:19 PM||||I have the Formula all back together, everything went fairly smoothly, only a few bumps. Last night I connected all the wiring, hoses, exhuast, etc.  Then I installed my new master and slave cylinders for the clutch. I started bleeding the system and ,pop, the #### bleeder screw and its little housing for the threads came off! Its under warranty, I have checked all the local parts shops, no-one has one in stock.  It will take 7-30 days to get one in. WTF!!! This is supposed to happen to other people, not me! It had better take 1-2 days to even get me to reconsider this product maker again. If they screw up, and I don't get it, I'm postin' names.||||reg||8
Z||000001||DJRice||12-06-1999||04:21 PM||||Same thing happened to mine, but it wasn't new slave.  Its amazing what you can do with a propane torch and a whole lot of "want to" when you want to get a car on the road.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-06-1999||10:56 PM||||Looks like I'm too late. The garbage man already came and took my old slave.  Looks like I'm stuck without my car for <B> another </B> week or so. I do so terribly miss it. It sits there in the garage just looking so forlorne. sigh, maybe next week.<P>I've got a torch, but if I mess around with the slave waiting for the new one to come in, I don't think they'll accept it as a warranty item.  Too bad, I was considering using the torch. Oh, by the way, who ever suggested using a propane torch and a candle to free rusted bolts, excellent tip.  Took those rusty old bolts from my exhaust in no time.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-06-1999).]||||reg||1
